{"reference":"Isaiah 13","book":"Isaiah","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Reading Map","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"A commonly used reading map divides Isaiah into sections including chapters 1–12 and 13–27. On that map, Isaiah 13 begins a new major section. This is a practical way to navigate the book, not proof of a single uncontested large-scale structure or of a particular number of authors.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 13:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_013_399bedd40a044914368f159e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Isaiah Among the Nations","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Beyond this chapter, Isaiah addresses judgment involving Judah, Israel, and foreign nations. The book portrays empires as instruments that are also accountable, while also envisioning nations learning, worshiping, and sharing in restoration. This wider perspective places Babylon’s judgment within a book that does not treat any nation’s power as beyond judgment.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_013_3e39d9be9c5c26b6898f1cf3"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}